In the U.S., the Federal Reserve has signaled that it may begin to cut interest rates later this year, citing slowing inflation and weakening labor market data. However, some policymakers remain concerned about the risk of a recession. The ECB, on the other hand, has been more cautious, with some officials warning that premature rate cuts could lead to inflation resurging.

"Central banks are walking a tightrope," said economist Rajiv Patel. "They need to manage inflation without causing a downturn. The challenge is growing as global interdependencies increase."

